  "text": ".RowStyles\nAt the peak of a hill, there are two parallel roads \\( R \\) and \\( S \\). A creature named G looks from position \\( G \\) at the hill, where lines \\( B \\) and \\( C \\) form an angle of \\( 150^\\circ \\). If the creature moves to position \\( A \\), where \\( A \\), \\( B \\) and \\( C \\) form a triangle with angle \\( ABC = 270^\\circ \\), find the sum of angles \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\), both expressed in decimal degrees, where \\( B \\) is between \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\) (in degrees). Let's analyze the problem. There are two parallel roads \\( R \\) and \\( S \\) forming an angle of \\( 150^\\circ \\) at a peak of a hill. When G moves to position A, forming a triangle with \\( B \\) and \\( C \\), the new angle at \\( B \\) becomes \\( 270^\\circ \\). Angle \\( ABC = 270^\\circ \\) implies that it is effectively a straight angle, which means \\( B \\) and \\( C \\) are collinear.\n\nSince roads \\( R \\) and \\( S \\) are parallel, the angle \\( \\angle ABC \\) in the new position is a straight angle because G adjusted his orientation to conform to the new configuration.\n\nTherefore, both \\( B \\) and \\( C \\) lie on the same straight line. Since \\( B \\) and \\( Y \\) are on the same line and positioned such that \\( B \\) is between \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\), the given scenario suggests an angle geometry within the formed triangle AB C. However, it doesn't provide information on the exact orientation of \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\) from G's perspective at position \\( A \\) other than the angle \\( \\angle ABC \\) which was \\( 270^\\circ \\).\n\nSince the angle from G's position at A is \\( 270^\\circ \\) and assuming \\( G \\) is looking upwards towards \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\) in the formation of the triangle, the relative angles and configuration need to be determined.\n\nGiven that \\( G \\) moves from a higher angle of \\( 150^\\circ \\) to a \\( 270^\\circ \\) angle suggesting a smaller gap between \\( X \\), \\( Y \\), and \\( B \\) in the new setup, we contradict to find orientation of \\( B \\) to \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\).\n\nSince \\( \\angle ABC = 270^\\circ \\), if \\( G \\) does move downwards, then \\( 270^\\circ \\) corresponds to the existing street configuration participants G from \\( A \\). With no explicit information on relative position (X, Y), its makes sense to analyze the geometry.\n\nSo the key points:\n- Applied \\( \\angle ABC = 270^\\circ \\) conventionally.\n- The triangle vertex \\( A \\) advantage geometrical change (angle), no relative vector positions.\n\nSo sum SHOULD Figure this out:\n\\[ \\angle X = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABC = 180 - 270^\\circ = -90^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\angle Y = 270^\\circ \\text{ (directly from configurations listed)} \\]\n\nThus, finally:\n\\[ \\angle X + |\\angle Y| = 180^\\circ - 270 + 270 = 180^\\circ \\].\n\nFinal answer.\nboxed{180^\\circ }",
